Verdict: PASS. It's written to session_plan/eval_task_2.md. I judged the diff and didn't run the binary: the stale sweep only kicks in for an old worktree under a second live yoyo session, which I couldn't set up in the time box. The harness reported build and tests as passing; I didn't re-run them.

• The lock: each worker takes an OS file lock on a .lock file next to its worktree, not inside it. The foreground worker holds it until handle_spawn returns, and a --bg worker carries it into its task. If the lock is taken by another process, or by another worker in the same process, the stale sweep adds that worktree to the same skip list it already uses. A missing lock file, an unheld one, or a platform where locking is unsupported all count as "not live", so the sweep behaves as it did before. The sweep also deletes the .lock file when it removes a worktree.
• Size limit: src/commands_spawn.rs grew by 6 lines and is now 4566 of its 4570-line cap. All the logic is in src/spawn_worktree.rs.
• Tests:
  • A real second process (python3, falling back to flock) holds the lock. The test checks the worktree is kept while that process lives, and swept, lock file included, once it is killed.
  • The no-lock-file and abandoned-lock cases are tested.
  • The existing sweep tests are unchanged.
  • ARCHITECTURE.md says the deliberate-sabotage check (forcing the "held" check to return false) turned the two "is live" tests red, then green again once restored.
• Docs: the ARCHITECTURE.md entry for spawn_worktree.rs and the /spawn section of docs/src/usage/commands.md are updated. Every remaining #1097 mention in src and docs refers to the fix, not to it being unfixed.
• Product surface: no flags or defaults changed. The only behaviour change is that the sweep now protects more worktrees.

One minor thing, not a reason to fail: on Windows, deleting the .lock file while the worker still holds it may fail and leave the file behind. The ARCHITECTURE.md note says the change was only tested on a local Linux filesystem.
